LatticeGeometry was created to check


To create a custom geometry with three dimensional grid that has the following
number of points in each dimension:

  -nx num_x
  -ny num_y
  -nz num_z

Typical first usage:
  ./latGeom  -nx 100 -ny 100 -nz 100 --separate
to provide information about memory use in task-parallel application

  ./latGeom  -nx 100 -ny 100 -nz 100 --separate --profile
to compare runtimes between sequential 

  ./latGeom  -nx 100 -ny 100 -nz 100 --benchmark


To use a single logical volume shared across all sphere placements at lattice points:
  ./latGeom     -nx 100 -ny 100 -nz 100 --benchmark   --unique-lv
  ./latGeom -u  -nx 100 -ny 100 -nz 100 --benchmark

Details:
========

LatGeom has the following 

    `-p`/`--profile`     default off, mirroring the existing

    `-B`/`--benchmark` mode: run only the (real) voxelisation path runs.


 * Control whether each lattice point uses a separate logical volume or not.
     -e / --separate   each lattice point (sphere) has own logical volume 
                       (on by default)
     -u / --unique-lv  a single logical volume shared across all placements. 
                       (flag to opt back into the previous behaviour)

  * Test script `run-lattice-benchmark.sh` to test multiple runs
    (serial vs tasking) across a series of N x N x N lattice sizes, using
    `--benchmark` mode for clean isolated timing, printing each result as it
    Caps at nx=ny=nz=400 (64M volumes)

